class_name Logs
## Marlowe's recordings, 1–15. Found in band order; each teaches a real
## technique. Layer-one of the mystery surfaces across these fifteen:
## she wasn't salvaging — she was feeding something.

const BAND_LOGS := {
	1: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6],
	2: [7, 8, 9, 10, 11],
	3: [12, 13, 14, 15],
}

const ENTRIES := {
	1: {
		"title": "Log 01 — First Tide",
		"text": "Keeper's log, first tide. The lamp drinks oil, the line drinks light, and I drink whatever's left in the cellar. Rule one, whoever finds this: the line is honest. Everything you clip to your belt dims it. If the glow starts guttering, drop the heaviest thing you love — it will always be there tomorrow. You won't.",
	},
	2: {
		"title": "Log 02 — Breathing Holes",
		"text": "There's air down there, pockets of it, silvered against the ceilings of the kelp caves. The line brightens when you drink one. I map them like wells in a desert. Swim the ceilings, not the floors, when you're running dim.",
	},
	3: {
		"title": "Log 03 — Pinlights",
		"text": "Brushed an urchin bed today reaching for a coin purse. Felt the sting up the whole tether. The panic is worse than the poison — your eyes close in on you like a fist. Breathe. It passes quicker if you don't fight it. It passes quicker still if you never reach past what you can see.",
	},
	4: {
		"title": "Log 04 — The Green Idols",
		"text": "Found the first idol wedged under a wreck rib. Heavy as guilt. Carrying it, the line went from noon to dusk just like that. Here's the arithmetic nobody teaches you: a relic is worth a third of your dive. Decide BEFORE you pick it up whether it's worth a third of your life.",
	},
	5: {
		"title": "Log 05 — The Net Holds One Tide",
		"text": "Drowned my cargo once already — the net snagged on the drop where I fell. Went back down the very next tide and there it was, waiting, patient as a grave. It won't wait longer than that. The sea forgives exactly once.",
	},
	6: {
		"title": "Log 06 — The Dredge",
		"text": "Something armored has claimed the sink at the bottom of the kelp. It charges anything that moves — blind fury, no cunning. But it HATES the stinging gardens. I watched it blunder into an anemone bed mid-charge and go rigid, eye out on its stalk like an offered pearl. If I wanted that eye, that's when I'd take it. At speed.",
	},
	7: {
		"title": "Log 07 — Ride the Water",
		"text": "The Middens. A whole town the sea swallowed and never chewed. The streets still run — as currents now, pouring through lanes and windows. Don't fight them; you'll spend your light and get nowhere. Enter where the water enters. Leave where it leaves. The town shows you its own doors.",
	},
	8: {
		"title": "Log 08 — Not Every Light Is a Friend",
		"text": "I followed a warm little glow into a cellar thinking it was one of my own flares. It had teeth. The lanternjaw wears a lamp the way I wear one, and it has learned what lamps mean to things like us. Study one from a safe dark corner, long enough to really SEE it — after that, its lie never works on you again.",
	},
	9: {
		"title": "Log 09 — Count to Two",
		"text": "The pipes have tenants. Eels, thick as my arm. They coil before they strike — there's a beat, a held breath, a count of two, then the lunge. Learn the coil and the pipe-riddled lanes become a rhythm game. Also: I've started leaving it parcels, down at the deep gate. Easier than the alternative. More on that when I'm braver.",
	},
	10: {
		"title": "Log 10 — The Anchor Trick",
		"text": "Best relic I own is a rusted anchor with a cargo ring. I sink it mid-dive, clip the haul to it, and swim on light as a prayer. Whatever the deep takes from me, it never takes what's on the anchor. It lets me pass, I think, because I keep bringing things down. It. I wrote 'it'. Leaving that in.",
	},
	11: {
		"title": "Log 11 — The Bell Still Swings",
		"text": "The brute in the church tower carries the old bell on its back like a saint's burden. Bare-handed you won't dent it. But the vents in that tower still breathe when you open their valves, and the water comes through hard enough to stagger a steeple. Give the water a push, put the brute in its path, and the bell rings it senseless. Then — the eye. Always the eye, always at speed.",
	},
	12: {
		"title": "Log 12 — Rooms the Light Refuses",
		"text": "Below the town: the Cathedral. There are pockets down there where my lamp just — stops. Not dims. Refuses. The dark in those rooms is older than the dark around it. Sound still works. Listen for the difference between water and stone, and walk the walls with your hands. You will want to rush. Don't.",
	},
	13: {
		"title": "Log 13 — Move Like Silt Settles",
		"text": "They sing in the nave. Pale things, robed in their own skin, and they are blind — utterly — but they hear a kick from three rooms away. I crossed the whole choir loft once, slow as silt settling, and they never turned. Speed is noise. Stillness is a hymn they can't hear. It's not salvage I bring down anymore. It's food. There. I've said it.",
	},
	14: {
		"title": "Log 14 — Kill Your Light",
		"text": "Try this near the Choir, if your nerve holds: kill your light. All of it. They drift toward warmth the way moths drift toward flame, and a dead lamp is a cold hymn to them. Doused and slow, I am nothing. They sing to something below the nave floor — the song goes DOWN, do you understand. It answers.",
	},
	15: {
		"title": "Log 15 — What I Actually Do Here",
		"text": "Let me be plain, whoever you are. I was never salvaging. The lighthouse ledger says 'recovered goods'; the truth is the goods go DOWN. I feed it. I have fed it for years, and it has let me keep this little circle of light in exchange. The cantor in the deep nave keeps the tally, and the pipes crack like bone when he strikes them — remember that, if you ever have to face him. I'm going to stop feeding it. Tomorrow. — M.",
	},
}
